#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on the Sāṃkhyakārikā, 2026-10-13
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:SamkhyaSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Sāṃkhyakārikā or Gauḍapāda names a sign by which the knowledge or the release would be recognised, by the knower or by another"@en, "Ob die Sāṃkhyakārikā oder Gauḍapāda ein Zeichen nennt, an dem das Wissen oder die Befreiung zu erkennen wäre, vom Wissenden oder von einem anderen"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Īśvarakṛṣṇa, Sāṃkhyakārikā, all seventy-two verses, in Colebrooke's translation of 1837 and Davies' of 1881, with the Sanskrit; Gauḍapāda's commentary at kārikās 1, 2, 23, 44 to 51, 64, 65, 67, 68 and 69 in Wilson's translation."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A mark, perception, bodily change, conduct or term by which the arising of the knowledge of verse 64 or the attainment of isolation of verse 68 would be known, before or after death, to the knower or to a bystander."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Sanskrit: liṅga, cihna, lakṣaṇa, dṛṣṭa, pratyakṣa, kāla, and the verbs of seeing and knowing in verses 64 to 68; in the English of both translations: sign, mark, token, perceive, see, behold, appear, body, time, and every conditional of verses 44 to 51 and 64 to 68."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-13" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-13" ;
    skos:note "Nothing that a bystander could use, and one verse that rules it out. Verse 65 says that the witness, possessed of the knowledge, beholds nature at ease as a spectator; that is a perception of the knower and is stated as the content of the knowledge, not as its sign. Verse 67 says that the knower keeps a body by the force of past impressions, entered at pmc:SamkhyaDhrtasarira, so that his continued life is no evidence either way; verse 68 puts the release at the separation from the body. The word liṅga occurs in the work in its technical sense, the subtle body and the mark of inference, and in no verse as a sign of attainment."@en .

pmp:SamkhyaFailureSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Sāṃkhyakārikā or Gauḍapāda states under what conditions the study of the principles fails"@en, "Ob die Sāṃkhyakārikā oder Gauḍapāda angibt, unter welchen Bedingungen die Einübung der Prinzipien scheitert"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same verses and the same passages of the commentary."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated condition under which the practice does not bring the knowledge or the knowledge does not bring release, and whether such a condition is marked by anything observable."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "viparyaya, aśakti, tuṣṭi, aṅkuśa, bandha, saṃsāra, prakṛtilaya, in the Sanskrit; obstruction, error, disability, incapacity, acquiescence, contentment, curb, check, bondage, not liberated, migrates anew, does not obtain, in the English of Colebrooke, Davies and Wilson."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:SamkhyaTusti, pmc:SamkhyaPrakrtilayaPunarbhava, pmc:SamkhyaSiddhiAnkusa ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-13" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-13" ;
    skos:note "Two conditions are stated in so many words, both in the commentary: dispassion without knowledge of the principles brings absorption into nature and rebirth, and contentment in time or in luck brings no liberation. The kārikā itself gives the general form, that error, incapacity and contentment are the curb of the perfections. All three locate the failure in the practitioner's belief; the eleven injuries of the organs among the incapacities are the only listed causes a second person could observe, and the text does not say that they prevent the knowledge, only that they count among the fifty forms."@en .
